  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q25-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Consider the linear equation $ax+b=c$. Can you find values of $a$, $b$ and $c$ for which this equation has:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p7-q25-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "no solution",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $a=0$, $b=1$, $c=2$.\n\nChoose $a=0$ and $b\\ne c$.",
          "worked": "Choose $a=0$, $b=1$, $c=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}0x+1&=2\\\\1&=2\\end{align*}$$\nThis is false for every $x$, so there is no solution. In general, choose $a=0$ and $b\\ne c$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q25-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "more than one solution",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $a=0$, $b=1$, $c=1$.\n\nChoose $a=0$ and $b=c$; every $x$ is a solution.",
          "worked": "Choose $a=0$, $b=1$, $c=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}0x+1&=1\\\\1&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThis is true for every $x$, so there are infinitely many solutions. In general, choose $a=0$ and $b=c$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q25-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "exactly one solution",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $a=1$, $b=0$, $c=2$, giving $x=2$.\n\nAny $a\\ne0$ gives exactly one solution, $x=\\dfrac{c-b}{a}$.",
          "worked": "Choose $a=1$, $b=0$, $c=2$. The equation becomes $x=2$, which has exactly one solution.\nFor any $a\\ne0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}ax+b&=c\\\\ax&=c-b\\\\x&=\\frac{c-b}{a}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q25-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "exactly two solutions?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Impossible.\n\nIf $a\\ne0$, there is one solution. If $a=0$, there are either no solutions or infinitely many.",
          "worked": "If $a\\ne0$, solving gives the single value $x=\\dfrac{c-b}{a}$.\nIf $a=0$, the equation becomes $b=c$: it is true for every $x$ when $b=c$, and false for every $x$ when $b\\ne c$.\nTherefore, exactly two solutions are impossible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
